# TestPilot Interview Preparation Guide

This guide turns TestPilot into an interview-ready project story. It is written to match the project section of the resume while staying accurate to the implementation.

## 1. Best opening introduction

### One-line version

> TestPilot is a full-stack developer productivity tool that converts Chrome Recorder journeys into maintainable, executable Playwright TypeScript projects.

### 30-second version

> I built TestPilot to remove the repetitive work between recording a browser flow and creating a maintainable automation project. A user imports Chrome Recorder JSON, and TestPilot validates and normalizes the commands, ranks the captured locators by quality, protects sensitive values, and deterministically generates a Playwright project with a Page Object, test specification, configuration, package metadata, and environment template. The same project can be previewed, executed locally in Chromium from the web interface, and exported as a ZIP. I also built a bounded repair lab that can diagnose a controlled locator-drift failure, select a safe fallback from the original recording, patch only that locator, and verify one retry.

### 90-second version

> TestPilot is a local full-stack automation studio built with React, TypeScript, Express, Zod, and Playwright. The problem I wanted to solve was that Chrome Recorder captures useful browser interaction evidence, but its raw JSON is not the same as a maintainable test framework. Engineers still have to interpret commands, choose stable selectors, create Page Objects, configure Playwright, handle secrets, run the test, and package the result.
>
> TestPilot turns that manual sequence into one pipeline. The frontend imports a Recorder JSON file and sends it through a typed normalization layer. That layer maps raw commands into a smaller internal model, removes redundant events such as `keyUp`, identifies unsupported actions, converts Recorder selectors into Playwright locator candidates, and assigns deterministic quality scores. A pure code-generation engine then creates six files: an executable test, a Page Object, Playwright configuration, package metadata, an environment template, and a README.
>
> The React studio lets the user inspect every normalized step, change the preferred locator, edit non-sensitive values, preview all generated files, choose trace, screenshot, and video settings, and export the project. For live validation, the Express backend validates the payload again, writes an isolated run directory, launches Playwright Chromium as a child process, and returns status, progress, console output, and retained failure artifacts.
>
> I also added a deliberately constrained repair workflow. When a controlled UI mutation breaks a role locator, the agent reads Playwright failure evidence, identifies the failed recorded step, chooses a non-XPath fallback with a score of at least 70 from the original recording, changes only that locator, and retries once. It is deterministic and auditable rather than an unrestricted self-healing system.

## 2. The problem TestPilot solves

Chrome DevTools Recorder is useful for capturing a user journey, but a recording alone has several limitations:

1. Raw commands are not organized as a reusable automation framework.
2. Recorder JSON may contain several locator alternatives of very different quality.
3. Recorded passwords or tokens can accidentally enter generated source code.
4. Generated actions may not follow application-specific interaction requirements.
5. A developer still needs a Playwright config, test structure, Page Object, dependencies, and documentation.
6. There is no project-specific review screen for unsupported commands or weak selectors.
7. Exporting code without executing it can produce a project that looks correct but fails immediately.
8. Locator drift remains a common cause of UI automation failures.

TestPilot provides one local workflow for all of those steps:

```text
Record -> Import -> Validate -> Normalize -> Score -> Inspect
       -> Generate -> Preview -> Execute -> Diagnose -> Export
```

The key value is not just code conversion. It is turning recorder evidence into a reviewable, testable, and portable automation project.

## 3. What the product does

| Capability | What the user sees | What happens internally |
|---|---|---|
| Import | Select or drag a Recorder JSON file | Browser parses JSON and Zod validates the recording shape |
| Normalize | A sequential execution map | Raw commands become a typed internal step model |
| Locator review | Ranked selector alternatives and quality scores | Selector syntax is classified and scored deterministically |
| Value editing | Editable non-sensitive input values | The in-memory normalized step is updated and code is regenerated |
| Secret protection | Sensitive values display as protected | Recorded value becomes `{{TESTPILOT_SECRET}}` and generated code reads an environment variable |
| Code preview | Six generated files shown in the browser UI | A shared pure TypeScript generator rebuilds the project from current state |
| Live run | Status, duration, completed steps, logs, and artifacts | Express writes a run project and starts Playwright Chromium locally |
| Demo run | Predictable step-by-step simulated output | No external browser or website is contacted |
| Repair lab | Failure, diagnosis, locator diff, and verified retry | A bounded policy selects one recorded fallback and retries once |
| Export | Downloadable ZIP | Express streams all generated files through Archiver |

## 4. What TestPilot does not claim to be

Use these boundaries when an interviewer probes the project:

- It is not a cloud-hosted multi-tenant platform. It is a local single-user developer tool.
- It is not a general autonomous coding agent.
- It does not let an LLM freely rewrite tests.
- Its locator score is a heuristic quality signal, not proof that a selector is unique or valid at runtime.
- Its repair lab handles one controlled category: locator drift with a previously recorded safe fallback.
- It does not support every Chrome Recorder command. Unsupported commands are surfaced for manual conversion.
- The browser UI initiates execution, but the Playwright process runs in the local Node backend, not inside the browser sandbox.
- The generated output is a ready-to-run project foundation. Organization-specific fixtures, authentication, test data, and CI policies may still need to be added.

Those constraints make the project easier to defend because they show deliberate engineering judgment rather than overclaiming.

